  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "The Producer Price Index (PPI) measures the average change over time in the selling prices received by domestic producers for their output, acting as a leading indicator of inflation before it reaches consumers. In February 2026, U.S. wholesale prices rose 0.7% (3.4% annually), exceeding forecasts. Key Details on the PPI: Purpose: It tracks price changes from the perspective of the seller (producer), covering goods, services, and construction. February 2026 Data: The 0.7% monthly increase was primarily driven by a 0.5% rise in services. Core PPI (excluding volatile food and energy) rose 0.5%. Significance: A rising PPI indicates rising input costs for businesses, which can lead to higher consumer prices, while falling PPI suggests weak demand. Core PPI (Core Inflation): Excluding food and energy, core PPI rose 3.9% year-over-year, indicating persistent inflationary pressure, notes Yahoo Finance. Components: The PPI covers final demand goods, services, and construction, with services making up about 41% of the index, say Trading Economics. This data was released by the Bureau of Labor Statistics (BLS).",
